  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 2035(a), if a decedent transfers a life insurance policy within 3 years of death, the full death benefit is included in the gross estate. The taxpayer died 2 years after the transfer, triggering the 3-year rule, so the entire $2 million is included. Had the decedent survived beyond 3 years, the proceeds would have been excluded.
Wrong (A): Irrevocability alone does not exclude the proceeds. The 3-year lookback rule under Sec. 2035(a) overrides the trust''s irrevocable status for transfers of life insurance incidents of ownership.
Wrong (C): Only including the cash surrender value at the date of transfer is incorrect. Sec. 2035(a) requires inclusion of the full death benefit, not the value at the time of transfer.
Wrong (D): Although the trust owned the policy at death, the 3-year rule brings the proceeds back into the estate because the decedent transferred incidents of ownership within the lookback period.$EXPL$
