-- Q4026: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 2503(b), only present interests qualify for the annual gift tax exclusion. Gifts to an irrevocable trust are generally future interests. Crummey withdrawal powers — named after Crummey v. Commissioner (397 F.2d 82, 9th Cir. 1968) — convert future interests to present interests by giving beneficiaries a temporary right (typically 30 to 60 days) to withdraw newly contributed amounts.
Wrong (A): A spendthrift clause restricts beneficiary access and would make the gift a future interest, the opposite of what is needed to qualify for the annual exclusion.
Wrong (B): Naming the CPA as trustee has no bearing on whether the gift qualifies as a present interest under Sec. 2503(b). Trustee identity does not affect gift tax exclusion eligibility.
Wrong (C): Mandatory current income distribution is not required for the annual exclusion in a dynasty trust context. Crummey powers, not income distribution requirements, are the standard mechanism.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4026;

